Z||000000||Dan86gt3.8||02-24-2000||04:49 PM||||YES I WAS WONDERING HOW MUCH CLEARENCE THERE IS BETWEEN THE FRAME (CRADLE) AND THE PART OF THE TRANNY WHERE THE CV AXLE SNAPS IN ON THE PASSENGER SIDE??  ALSO HAVE ANY OF YOU HAD CLEARENCE PROBLEMS WITH THE AXLE ON THE PASSENGER SIDE.  WE HAVE HAD PROBLEMS WHEN WE FIRST PUT IT IN, WE ADDED A DOGBONE THAT STOPPED SOME OF IT, THEN I GOT ON IT LAST FRIDAY NIGHT AND THE AXLE CAME APPART WHERE THE CLOVER SHAPED JOINT FITS INTO THE CUP THAT SNAPS INTO THE TRANNY.  DOES ANYONE KNOW HOW MUCH ROOM THERE IS AROUND THERES?  OR BETTER YET DOES ANYONE HAVE PICS OF THAT AREA WITH THE TIRE OFF?  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||02-24-2000||05:08 PM||||are you jamming the inner CV or over extending it? Ideally you want to make the axle up so that the spider is centered in the races on the inner CV.<P>Since you now have a wrecked one it's easy to work it out. If you've already torn it appart put it back together minus the inner boot. If it's bottoming then you need to shorten. if it's not at least 1/2 way in, With The Car at Ride Hight at rest, then it must be made longer. To make up for the lost needles wrap the spider with tape then put the donuts back on.<P>If this is happening because of engine movement then you also need to carefully look at how you did the mounts. It sounds like they are very poorly setup or you did the axle wrong in the first place.<P>For complete testing you would want the spring off the strut. (This can be done w/o screwing up the alignment. Don't take the casting off the strut.)((You could compress the spring in place, but if a compressor slips it can do major damage as it flys across the yard.)) then you can easily raise and lowwer the car to check the amount of tavel in the inner CV is correct as the outer end moves. The inner CV should stay in the middle 1/3 of it's total travel most of the time. Occasional travel to the 1/4 lines is ok. more than that and you risk blowing it appart or bottoming the cup.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Dan86gt3.8||02-24-2000||05:23 PM||||well I was turning left and was hard on the throttle, I intially thought that the rear end was gona slide on me but then there was a pop and it reved way up.  SO I am guessing that the car pushing to right (counter clockwise spin would cause the axle to compress fully.  Also I don't think when we mounted the engine we werent concerend enough with the tranny and where more concerend with the main pulley being in propper position.  Anyways what you said makes sinse I will look at it, thanks.<P>ps- do you have a 3.8?||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||02-24-2000||07:10 PM||||Those are the guidlines for working out any half shaft with any tranny.<P>You very likely have a mount and/or suspension problem in addition to what ever else. Again. you need to determine wether it pulled out or bottomed. If it bottomed there will be marks all over in the cup and a broken spider. If it pulled out there will be damage at the mouth of the cup and parts everywhere. The spider will have little or no damage if it pulled out.<P>You must get this grease off the belts. you should replace a serpent belt and clean the pulleys with solvent. Any grease or oil at all on a serpent ruins it in a few miles. Seperpents are hard to clean well. Maybe some orange(citrus base) cleaner would work on them.||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||02-24-2000||07:19 PM||||Did you account for the longer case on the 4T60E over the 4T60 and 125C? The axle needs to be shorter on the passenger side. Did you recenter the engine to account for this or just let the inner CV slider further in.<P>I put this combo in a Ciera and I had to swap that shaft or the inner CV just about bottomed out. Luckily, there are shaft combinations that allow this.<P>Terry||||reg||1
